WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:25.200
Previously on Practical Prophets... Moses was dead, and Joshua took command at the Jordan. Rahab protected two Israelite scouts in Jericho and secured a promise for her family. God stopped the swollen river, and Israel crossed into the land. At Gilgal they renewed the covenant, celebrated Passover, and ate the land's harvest as the manna came to an end. Now Joshua faces Jericho.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.650
Chapter 6. A City That Would Not Open.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:53.800
Jericho had shut itself. The city gates were closed against Israel. Nobody was coming in, and nobody was going out. Whatever the inhabitants knew of Joshua's army, of the Jordan, or of the God of Israel, their response was to seal the walls.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:01:14.480
Joshua's first target was therefore a fortified city whose defenders intended to stay behind stone. God told him that Jericho, its king, and its fighting men had been delivered into his hands. But the instructions that followed were not a plan of siege ramps or battering engines.

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:35.135
The Israelite warriors were to walk around the city once each day for six days. Seven priests would carry seven ram's horns before the Ark of the Covenant. On the seventh day they would circle the city seven times. At the final, long blast of the horns, the entire people would shout.

00:01:35.315 --> 00:01:56.265
Then, God said, the wall would collapse. Joshua called the priests and relayed the command. The Ark was to be carried in the procession; armed men would go before it, and a rear guard behind it. The priests would sound the ram's horns as they moved. Joshua added one instruction for the rest of the people.

00:01:56.445 --> 00:02:16.470
They were not to shout. They were not even to speak until he gave the signal. So the procession moved around Jericho in the unmistakable presence of its defenders. Soldiers in front, priests sounding horns, the Ark in their midst, an armed guard at the rear—and the rest of the people silent.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:38.175
After a single circuit they returned to camp. The next morning Joshua rose early. The priests lifted the Ark, and the procession went again. One circuit, then back to camp. The same thing happened for six days. The city remained shut. On the seventh morning, at dawn, the Israelites began once more.

00:02:38.355 --> 00:02:54.555
But this time the procession did not return after one circuit. It went around twice, then again, until it had circled the city seven times. At the seventh circuit the priests blew their horns, and Joshua gave the order. “Shout.

00:02:54.735 --> 00:03:12.710
The Lord has given you the city.” But before the city fell, Joshua told Israel what victory would mean. Jericho was to be placed under cherem—devoted to destruction. The people were not to treat its possessions as ordinary spoils.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:31.865
They must not take what was devoted, because doing so would bring the consequences of the ban upon Israel's own camp. There was one exception to the destruction: Rahab, the woman who had sheltered Joshua's two scouts, and the people gathered in her house under the terms of their oath.

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:52.245
Silver, gold, and vessels of bronze and iron were not personal prizes either. They were to be set apart for the sanctuary treasury. Then the horns sounded and the people shouted. The city wall fell. Israel went straight into Jericho and took it. The account does not disguise what followed.

00:03:52.425 --> 00:04:14.850
The inhabitants—men and women, young and old—and the domestic animals were killed with the sword. It is one of the book's harshest scenes. The narrative presents the destruction as the execution of cherem, not as a bloodless occupation. Joshua had not forgotten the separate promise made to Rahab.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:33.830
He told the same two men who had entered her house as scouts to go back and bring her out with everyone belonging to her, as they had sworn. They did. Rahab's father, mother, brothers, and wider family came out with her. Initially they were placed outside Israel's camp.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:48.635
The narrative later reports that Rahab lived among Israel and remained there “to this day.” The city itself was burned. Only the dedicated metals were preserved for the treasury of the house of God.

00:04:48.815 --> 00:05:10.440
Joshua then pronounced an oath-bound curse on anyone who would rebuild Jericho: such a man would lay its foundations at the cost of his firstborn and establish its gates at the cost of his youngest son. The chapter closes with the report that God was with Joshua and that his reputation spread through the land.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:21.745
It was a victory. But the details were far stranger, and far more morally demanding, than a conventional account of an army capturing its first city.

00:05:22.445 --> 00:05:43.470
The Hebrew word cherem is crucial here. It marks things devoted to God and barred from ordinary private use; in this narrative that devotion includes the destruction of the city and its inhabitants. A responsible retelling should not soften that violence or pretend it was a conventional military occupation.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:59.325
Later Jewish sources discuss the moral and legal dimensions of conquest, but Joshua 6 itself presents the destruction directly. The chapter also completes Rahab's story: the scouts honor the oath, and she is brought into Israel.

00:05:59.505 --> 00:06:10.330
Notice the curse on rebuilding Jericho; its formulation involving the firstborn and youngest son matters again later in the biblical historical narrative.

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:14.230
Chapter 7. Thirty-Six Dead.

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:34.755
The victory at Jericho created a dangerous expectation: perhaps the next city would fall as quickly as the first. There was a fact Joshua did not yet know. During the destruction of Jericho, a man from the tribe of Judah had secretly taken items under cherem.

00:06:34.935 --> 00:06:55.310
His name was Achan, son of Carmi, descended from Zabdi and Zerah. What he took was not supposed to belong to anyone in Israel. The narrator reveals the theft before Joshua learns about it. That matters, because the defeat that follows is otherwise almost incomprehensible.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:07:16.565
Joshua sent scouts from Jericho to Ai, near Beth-aven, east of Bethel. The scouts returned with encouraging advice. Ai was small, they said; there was no reason to send the entire army. Two or three thousand men should be enough. Joshua sent approximately three thousand. They fled.

00:07:16.745 --> 00:07:37.720
The men of Ai killed about thirty-six Israelites, chasing the survivors from the city gate toward Shebarim and striking them on the descent. The effect on Israel was immediate. The book uses the same language once applied to frightened Canaanites: the people's hearts melted and became like water.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:57.400
Joshua tore his clothes, fell before the Ark, and stayed face down until evening. Israel's elders joined him, putting dust on their heads in mourning. His prayer was raw. Why, he asked God, had Israel been brought across the Jordan only to be handed over to the Amorites?

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:17.480
Would it have been better to remain east of the river? How could Joshua explain this humiliation? If the Canaanites heard of it, they would surround Israel and erase its name. And what, he demanded, would become of God's own reputation? God's answer was blunt. “Get up.” Israel had sinned.

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:34.085
Someone had broken the covenant by taking what was devoted, stealing it, hiding the theft, and placing the forbidden objects among ordinary possessions. Israel could not withstand its enemies while this violation remained unaddressed.

00:08:34.265 --> 00:08:56.465
Unless the devoted things were removed, God's presence would no longer protect the camp. Joshua was told to have the people consecrate themselves. The next day the guilty party would be identified through a procedure narrowing tribe by tribe, clan by clan, household by household, and finally person by person.

00:08:56.645 --> 00:09:20.045
The penalty announced was terrible: the offender and what belonged to him were to be destroyed by fire. The next morning Joshua began. Judah was selected. Within Judah, the Zerahite clan. Then Zabdi's household. Finally Achan son of Carmi. Joshua addressed him as “my son” and urged him to honor God by telling the truth.

00:09:20.225 --> 00:09:38.000
Achan confessed. He had seen a splendid cloak from Shinar, two hundred shekels of silver, and a gold bar weighing fifty shekels. He wanted them, so he took them. The valuables lay buried in his tent, with the silver underneath. Joshua sent messengers.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:52.230
They ran to the tent and found the hidden objects exactly where Achan said they would be. The items were brought before Joshua and all Israel and laid out before God. Then came the punishment.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:10:05.835
Achan, the stolen goods, his sons and daughters, his livestock, his tent, and all he possessed were taken to the Valley of Achor. Joshua confronted him: “Why have you brought trouble on us?

00:10:06.015 --> 00:10:25.490
The Lord will bring trouble upon you today.” The text reports that Israel stoned him, and that burning and further stoning followed. The phrasing and the inclusion of his family raise difficult questions about who died and how later commentators understand collective punishment.

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:45.945
Those questions cannot be answered by quietly removing the family from the scene: they are explicitly listed in the narrative. A great heap of stones was raised. The place became known as the Valley of Achor—the Valley of Trouble. Only then, says the account, did God's anger turn away.

00:10:46.125 --> 00:11:05.450
At Jericho, Israel had been told that victory was not permission to take everything. At Ai, the army learned that one hidden act could transform the nation's next battle. The disaster had not begun at Ai's walls. It had begun in the spoils of Jericho.

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:21.300
This chapter is designed as a mystery whose answer the reader already knows. The text names Achan at the start while Joshua remains unaware of him. The place-name Achor echoes the Hebrew root for troubling or bringing disaster.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:36.580
The punishment is morally difficult: the narrative lists Achan's children along with his property, and the phrasing of the execution is not straightforward. Traditional commentators differ on the details and responsibilities involved.

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:47.610
The soundest approach is to preserve what the text says and present interpretation as interpretation, rather than pretending those complications do not exist.

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:51.360
Chapter 8. The Trap at Ai.

00:11:52.460 --> 00:12:10.760
Joshua had sent a small force to Ai and watched it flee. Now God told him to return—and not to be afraid. This time Joshua was to bring the fighting force, not merely a few thousand soldiers. Ai and its king would be given into his hands.

00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:32.015
The city would be destroyed, as Jericho had been, but there was a crucial difference: Israel was now permitted to keep Ai's livestock and material spoils. The thing Achan had stolen at Jericho would not have been forbidden here. The law of the campaign, not simple appetite, determined what Israel could take.

00:12:32.195 --> 00:12:54.270
Joshua's plan depended on the enemy remembering Israel's previous defeat. He selected a force of thirty thousand capable soldiers and sent them out by night with orders to hide west of Ai, between Ai and Bethel. His instructions were precise. Joshua and the main force would approach the city.

00:12:54.450 --> 00:13:16.525
When Ai's defenders came out, Israel would pretend to break and retreat. The men of Ai would pursue, expecting another easy victory. Once they were drawn away, the ambush would rise, seize the undefended city, and set it on fire. Joshua sent the first force to its position and spent the night with the people.

00:13:16.705 --> 00:13:28.055
The next morning he mustered the army and advanced toward Ai with Israel's elders. They took position north of the city, with a valley between them and the walls.

00:13:28.235 --> 00:13:47.685
The narrative also describes a force of about five thousand stationed in ambush to the west; the relationship between this figure and the earlier thirty thousand has been discussed by commentators, and both details belong to the account. Joshua went into the valley that night.

00:13:47.865 --> 00:14:07.040
At the appointed time the king of Ai saw Israel's movement and marched out with his men. He did not know an ambush waited behind the city. Joshua's forces turned and fled in the direction of the wilderness. It was exactly what the defenders expected. Ai's men pursued.

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:30.795
The text adds that men went out from both Ai and Bethel, leaving the city open behind them. Then God told Joshua to extend the javelin in his hand toward Ai. He raised it. The hidden soldiers rose, raced into the city, seized it, and set it on fire. The men of Ai looked back and saw smoke rising from their own city.

00:14:30.975 --> 00:14:51.425
Suddenly their triumph made no sense. The Israelite army that had been running away turned to face them. The ambushers emerged behind them. Ai's forces were trapped between two Israelite groups. The account says that no one escaped. The king was captured alive and brought to Joshua.

00:14:51.605 --> 00:15:15.180
Israel then returned to Ai itself and killed its inhabitants. The number given for those who died that day, men and women, is twelve thousand. Joshua kept his javelin extended until the destruction was complete. This time the livestock and spoils were taken, as God had permitted. Ai was burned and left in ruins.

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:33.035
The captured king was hung on a tree until evening; at sunset his body was taken down and placed at the entrance of the city gate beneath a heap of stones. The military disaster had been reversed. But the chapter did not end at the battlefield.

00:15:33.215 --> 00:15:53.265
Joshua built an altar to Israel's God on Mount Ebal, following Moses' instructions. It was made of uncut stones untouched by iron tools. Israel offered burnt offerings and peace offerings there. Joshua wrote a copy of Moses' law on stones in the sight of the people.

00:15:53.445 --> 00:16:15.945
Then he assembled all Israel around the Ark: elders, officers, judges, priests, people born into Israel and the foreigners living among them. Half stood toward Mount Gerizim and half toward Mount Ebal, according to the arrangement Moses had commanded. Joshua read aloud the blessings and curses of the Torah.

00:16:16.125 --> 00:16:38.150
The narrative emphasizes the completeness of the occasion. He omitted none of Moses' commands. Women, children, and the resident foreigners heard the reading along with the rest of the assembly. The army had learned to defeat Ai by using the memory of its earlier defeat. But the public ceremony made a different point.

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:44.580
Victory in Canaan did not relieve Israel of the covenant that had brought them there.

00:16:45.280 --> 00:17:00.655
The description of the ambush uses two numbers—thirty thousand and five thousand—and presents movements on different nights. Readers should know that this is a textual difficulty, not silently flatten it into one uncomplicated figure.

00:17:00.835 --> 00:17:20.685
The second half of the chapter matters just as much as the battle: the altar of uncut stones, the written Torah, and the recitation of blessing and curse on Ebal and Gerizim fulfill instructions given by Moses in Deuteronomy. The complete assembly includes women, children, and resident foreigners.

00:17:20.865 --> 00:17:27.940
A retelling that ended at Ai's destruction would miss the chapter's deliberate return from warfare to covenant.

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:31.790
Chapter 9. The Bread That Lied.

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:54.690
Once the news of Jericho and Ai spread, the rulers of Canaan began to calculate their chances. Kings from the hill country, the lowlands, and the Mediterranean coast—including the Hittite, Amorite, Canaanite, Perizzite, Hivite, and Jebusite territories—formed a common front against Joshua and Israel.

00:17:54.870 --> 00:18:15.945
The people of Gibeon chose another strategy. They would make Israel believe they had come from far away. The Gibeonites knew what had happened to Jericho and Ai. Instead of marching to battle, they sent representatives to the Israelite camp at Gilgal disguised as travelers who had spent weeks on the road.

00:18:16.125 --> 00:18:35.525
The props were carefully chosen. Old sacks hung from their donkeys. Their wineskins were cracked, patched, and worn. Their shoes and clothing were battered. Their bread had dried into crumbs. They came to Joshua and Israel's leaders with a request: “We have come from a distant country.

00:18:35.705 --> 00:18:55.155
Make a covenant with us.” The Israelites saw the problem immediately. What if these men lived nearby? How could Israel make such a treaty with inhabitants of the very land it had been commanded to take? The visitors did not offer a verifiable answer. “We are your servants,” they said.

00:18:55.335 --> 00:19:16.185
Joshua pressed them: Who were they, and where had they come from? From far away, they insisted. They had heard what Israel's God had done in Egypt and how Sihon of Heshbon and Og of Bashan had been defeated east of the Jordan. Their elders had sent them to negotiate because of God's reputation.

00:19:16.365 --> 00:19:36.540
Then they produced the evidence of their journey. The bread had been hot when they left home, they said. Now it was hard and dry. The wineskins had been new, the shoes sound, the clothing intact. Look what distance had done to them. The leaders examined the provisions. And they made the decisive mistake.

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:58.045
The text records it in a single understated sentence: they did not seek counsel from God. Joshua made peace with the visitors. The leaders of Israel swore an oath that they would be allowed to live. Three days later, the deception unraveled. The strangers were not distant travelers at all. They were neighbors.

00:19:58.225 --> 00:20:16.000
Israel reached their cities: Gibeon, Chephirah, Beeroth, and Kiriath-jearim. The Israelite people were furious. They complained against the leaders who had made the agreement. But the leaders answered that they had sworn an oath in the name of Israel's God.

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:34.855
Killing the Gibeonites now would violate that oath and bring divine anger upon Israel. The treaty would stand. There would be a consequence, however. The Gibeonites would live, but they would be assigned to cut wood and draw water for the community. Joshua called them to account.

00:20:35.035 --> 00:20:53.635
Why had they lied about coming from a distant country when they lived among Israel? He pronounced a curse upon their future status: there would always be men from their people assigned to gather wood and carry water for the house of God. The Gibeonites did not deny the deception.

00:20:53.815 --> 00:21:13.640
They explained that they had heard of God's command to Moses to give Israel the land and destroy its inhabitants. They had feared for their lives. They had done what they could to survive. They now placed themselves in Joshua's hands. He might do what seemed right to him. Joshua kept them alive.

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:32.570
He rescued them from the Israelites who wanted to kill them and assigned them the promised service for the congregation and the altar, in the place God would choose. Two stories now stood beside each other. Rahab had protected Israel's agents and bargained for her family's survival.

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:51.425
The Gibeonites had tricked Israel into granting them survival. In both cases, people from the land reached an agreement with Israel rather than fight it. But only in the second case had Joshua's leaders been deceived. The lesson was not merely that enemies could be clever.

00:21:51.605 --> 00:21:57.555
It was that a sworn commitment could bind even those who had made it unwisely.

00:21:58.255 --> 00:22:15.905
The hinge of the entire chapter is verse 14: the Israelites examined the visitors' food but “did not ask counsel at the mouth of the Lord.” It is a concise criticism of leaders relying on plausible evidence without seeking the guidance the narrative expects of them.

00:22:16.085 --> 00:22:34.735
Yet after discovering the deception, they refuse to violate their oath. The book places two obligations in tension rather than offering an easy escape. The four Gibeonite cities are named, and the labor sentence is tied specifically to service for the congregation and God's altar.

00:22:35.635 --> 00:22:38.960
Chapter 10. The Day the Sun Stood Still.

00:22:40.060 --> 00:22:59.935
The people of Gibeon had survived by making a treaty with Israel. Their neighbors did not consider that a harmless choice. Adoni-zedek, king of Jerusalem, heard that Jericho and Ai had fallen and that Gibeon had made peace with Joshua. Gibeon was not a minor village.

00:23:00.115 --> 00:23:22.115
It was a considerable city, larger than Ai, with capable fighting men. Its decision to side with Israel threatened the other Canaanite rulers. The king of Jerusalem sent to four kings—Hoham of Hebron, Piram of Jarmuth, Japhia of Lachish, and Debir of Eglon—with a proposal: attack Gibeon.

00:23:22.295 --> 00:23:43.020
Five Amorite kings assembled their armies and surrounded the city. The Gibeonites sent an urgent message to Joshua's camp at Gilgal. They addressed him as their protector and begged him not to abandon his servants. The kings of the hill country had united against them; Joshua must come quickly.

00:23:43.200 --> 00:24:01.875
Joshua had been deceived into making the treaty. Now he faced the cost of honoring it. He took his fighting men from Gilgal, and God told him not to fear. The attacking kings had been delivered into Israel's hands; they would not prevail. Joshua marched through the night.

00:24:02.055 --> 00:24:23.330
He arrived unexpectedly, and the five-king coalition broke under the attack. Israel pursued the fleeing forces along the ascent of Beth-horon toward Azekah and Makkedah. The narrative describes God throwing great hailstones upon the fleeing men, killing more of them than Israel's soldiers killed with the sword.

00:24:23.510 --> 00:24:43.610
Then comes one of the most extraordinary passages in the book. In the presence of Israel, Joshua called for the sun to stand over Gibeon and the moon over the Valley of Aijalon. The account says that the sun stopped and the moon remained until Israel had finished dealing with its enemies.

00:24:43.790 --> 00:25:04.815
It refers readers to an older work called the Book of Jashar and describes a day unlike any before or since: God had listened to the voice of a human being, because God was fighting for Israel. The text briefly reports Joshua's return to Gilgal, then turns back to the fate of the five kings.

00:25:04.995 --> 00:25:23.170
They had fled and hidden inside a cave at Makkedah. When Joshua heard they had been found, he ordered great stones rolled across the entrance and guards stationed outside. But he would not allow the army to stop and celebrate a capture while the enemy was still fleeing.

00:25:23.350 --> 00:25:39.350
The soldiers were to continue the pursuit and prevent the survivors from reaching fortified cities. When the fighting ended, the Israelite soldiers returned safely to Joshua at Makkedah. The five kings were brought out of the cave.

00:25:39.530 --> 00:25:58.430
Joshua summoned Israel's commanders and ordered them to place their feet upon the kings' necks—a public sign of the rulers' defeat. He told the men not to be afraid or dismayed; they were to be strong and courageous, because God would do the same to the enemies they would face.

00:25:58.610 --> 00:26:18.960
The kings were executed and hung on five trees until evening. At sunset Joshua ordered the bodies taken down and placed inside the cave where they had hidden, with great stones sealed over the opening. The campaign continued through southern Canaan. Joshua took Makkedah, then Libnah.

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:39.815
He fought Lachish and captured it on the second day. Horam, king of Gezer, tried to help Lachish and was defeated. From there Joshua marched on Eglon, Hebron, and Debir, taking the cities and the surrounding settlements, and putting their inhabitants to the sword under the campaign's orders of destruction.

00:26:39.995 --> 00:26:56.720
The book surveys the southern theater: hill country, Negev, lowland, and slopes, from Kadesh-barnea toward Gaza and the region of Goshen to Gibeon. The narrative does not present these victories as quiet changes of government.

00:26:56.900 --> 00:27:19.850
It repeatedly describes killing, destruction, and the removal of opposition, and attributes Israel's victory to its God. Then Joshua and the army returned to Gilgal. This chapter began with the treaty Israel had been tricked into making. It ended with a large coalition defeated because Joshua honored that treaty.

00:27:20.030 --> 00:27:25.530
A pledge made through deception had become the occasion for a major war.

00:27:26.230 --> 00:27:35.605
Joshua's command to the sun and moon is quoted in poetic language and linked explicitly to the lost Sefer HaYashar, the Book of Jashar.

00:27:35.785 --> 00:27:50.910
Jewish commentators have interpreted the extraordinary day in different ways; the safest rendering is to state what Joshua says and what the biblical narrative reports, rather than pretending to establish an astronomical mechanism.

00:27:51.090 --> 00:28:10.565
Another detail worth preserving is the five kings' capture, imprisonment in a cave, later public humiliation, execution, and return to the cave. The campaign continues through named southern cities—Makkedah, Libnah, Lachish, Eglon, Hebron, Debir—and includes Gezer's intervention.

00:28:10.745 --> 00:28:17.370
Those are not interchangeable battle scenes; they establish the extent and severity of the campaign.

00:28:18.270 --> 00:28:21.295
Chapter 11. The Northern Kings.

00:28:22.395 --> 00:28:41.745
The fall of the southern coalition did not end the struggle for Canaan. In the north, Jabin, king of Hazor, had heard what Joshua had done. Hazor was an important regional center, and its ruler understood that Israel was no longer an isolated threat near the Jordan.

00:28:41.925 --> 00:28:56.550
He sent messages to Jobab of Madon, the rulers of Shimron and Achshaph, and the kings throughout the northern hill country, the Arabah south of Chinneroth, the western lowlands, and the region of Dor.

00:28:56.730 --> 00:29:16.505
The appeal extended across peoples and landscapes: Canaanites to the east and west, Amorites, Hittites, Perizzites, Jebusites in the hills, and Hivites in the territory below Mount Hermon near Mizpah. The response was enormous. The armies assembled at the waters of Merom.

00:29:16.685 --> 00:29:36.860
The text compares their numbers to sand on the seashore and emphasizes something Israel had not yet faced on this scale: numerous horses and chariots. God told Joshua not to fear their numbers. By the same time the next day, the opposing forces would be delivered over to Israel.

00:29:37.040 --> 00:30:00.440
Joshua was also instructed to hamstring their horses and burn their chariots. This was a striking command. Horses and chariots were symbols of military power. Israel was not to absorb the enemy's equipment as an easy route to a more formidable army. Joshua moved suddenly against the coalition at the waters of Merom.

00:30:00.620 --> 00:30:23.270
The enemy forces broke. Israel pursued them westward toward Great Sidon and Misrephoth-maim and east toward the Valley of Mizpah. The book reports that Joshua left none of their fighting forces able to continue resistance, then carried out the instructions concerning horses and chariots. Joshua turned back toward Hazor.

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:42.725
He captured the city, killed Jabin, and burned Hazor to the ground. The narrative pauses to explain why Hazor mattered: it had been the leading city among those northern kingdoms. Its destruction struck at the head of the coalition. Israel took the other kings' cities as well.

00:30:42.905 --> 00:30:55.580
The inhabitants were killed in the campaigns described, but the book draws one distinction. Most of the cities standing upon their mounds were not burned. Hazor was the exception.

00:30:55.760 --> 00:31:14.385
Spoils and livestock from the captured cities were taken by Israel, whereas the people were subjected to the destruction the text attributes to the commands given through Moses. The narrative then widens from one campaign to the whole war. Joshua fought for a long time.

00:31:14.565 --> 00:31:35.740
The conquered territory extended through the southern wilderness and hill country, the lowland, the Arabah, and the northern hills, from the mountain rising toward Seir to Baal-gad in the Lebanon Valley below Hermon. The text states that, apart from the Hivites of Gibeon, no city made peace with Israel.

00:31:35.920 --> 00:31:54.245
It also gives a theological interpretation of the resistance: God hardened the kings' hearts so that they would come out to battle and be destroyed. That is the narrator's theological explanation, not an independently observed account of what each ruler thought.

00:31:54.425 --> 00:32:17.975
Finally Joshua confronted the Anakim—the intimidating people associated with the earlier scouts' fear—from the highlands around Hebron, Debir, and Anab, and across the hill country of Judah and Israel. They were destroyed in those areas. The account notes a significant exception: some remained in Gaza, Gath, and Ashdod.

00:32:18.155 --> 00:32:39.655
Joshua had not made the land empty of every future adversary. The chapter's final statement belongs to the period it surveys: he took the land according to the promise made through Moses and allotted it to Israel by tribe. And the land had rest from war. It is an ending with the shape of closure.

00:32:39.835 --> 00:32:48.960
Yet the later chapters will show how much territory still remained to be possessed and how unfinished the reality of settlement was.

00:32:49.660 --> 00:33:03.410
The northern campaign is distinguished by horses and chariots, which are deliberately disabled or burned. Hazor, unlike most captured cities, is burned because of its status as the coalition's leading city.

00:33:03.590 --> 00:33:11.490
The phrase that Joshua fought “for a long time” prevents us from imagining the entire conquest as a few quick battles.

00:33:11.670 --> 00:33:25.920
And the book deliberately allows two perspectives to coexist: a summary that Joshua took the land and gave it rest, followed by detailed allotment chapters explaining that substantial possession remained incomplete.

00:33:26.100 --> 00:33:34.725
The Anakim survived in three coastal cities, including Gath, a name that becomes important later in biblical history.

00:33:35.625 --> 00:33:38.650
Chapter 12. Thirty-One Kings.

00:33:39.750 --> 00:33:56.675
After the accounts of battle, the book turns to a reckoning. What had begun as a handful of crossings and confrontations now had a geography. The land Israel claimed was measured in rivers, mountains, cities, and defeated kings.

00:33:56.855 --> 00:34:11.380
Joshua 12 names the rulers whose territories had passed into Israelite hands, beginning not with Joshua but with Moses. East of the Jordan, Moses and Israel had defeated Sihon of Heshbon.

00:34:11.560 --> 00:34:33.385
His territory stretched from Aroer at the Arnon Valley through part of Gilead toward the Jabbok, bordering Ammon; it also reached the Arabah, toward the Sea of Chinneroth and the Salt Sea, Beth-jeshimoth, and the slopes of Pisgah. Moses had also defeated Og of Bashan, described as a remnant of the Rephaim.

00:34:33.565 --> 00:34:55.015
Og's centers were Ashtaroth and Edrei; his rule reached Hermon, Salcah, Bashan and part of Gilead toward Geshur and Maacah. Moses had already given this eastern territory to Reuben, Gad, and half of Manasseh. This was the land whose warriors Joshua had called westward to help their brothers.

00:34:55.195 --> 00:35:16.870
The list then crossed the river. West of the Jordan, Joshua's victories ranged from Baal-gad in the Lebanon Valley to the barren mountain rising toward Seir. The book surveys hills, lowlands, desert, slopes, and the southern country, populated by the Hittites, Amorites, Canaanites, Perizzites, Hivites, and Jebusites.

00:35:17.050 --> 00:35:33.625
Then it names each defeated king. Jericho and Ai. Jerusalem and Hebron. Jarmuth, Lachish, Eglon, Gezer, Debir, Geder, Hormah, Arad, Libnah, Adullam, Makkedah, Bethel. Tappuah, Hepher, Aphek, and the Sharon.

00:35:33.805 --> 00:35:53.580
Madon, Hazor, Shimron-meron, Achshaph, Taanach, Megiddo, Kedesh, Jokneam near Carmel, Dor, Goiim in Gilgal, and Tirzah. Thirty-one kings in all. A catalogue is not usually the most dramatic part of a story. But there is a reason this one follows the fighting.

00:35:53.760 --> 00:36:12.410
The earlier chapters described victories as they happened, sometimes in extraordinary detail. The register converts them into a political account of what had changed. The kings had names; their territories had boundaries; and the land would have to be assigned.

00:36:12.590 --> 00:36:26.915
Yet the book's next move complicates the apparent completeness of this achievement. Joshua had defeated thirty-one kings west of the Jordan. That did not mean every part of the country had been occupied.

00:36:27.615 --> 00:36:47.940
This is a victory register, not thirty-one individual battle narratives. The names are part of the primary source; they should be preserved even when a listening version groups them rhythmically. Notice the division between territories acquired under Moses east of the Jordan and those acquired under Joshua west of it.

00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:58.795
The later allotment chapters are not mere paperwork after a completed conquest: they reveal how different gaining military dominance and living in the land could be.

00:36:59.695 --> 00:37:03.570
Chapter 13. The Map Was Not the Territory.

00:37:04.670 --> 00:37:23.295
Joshua was old. The book does not soften that fact. He had led Israel across the river, into battle, and through years of campaigning. Now God told him directly that he was advanced in years—and that there remained a great deal of land yet to be possessed.

00:37:23.475 --> 00:37:28.950
The admission comes immediately after the catalogue of defeated kings.

00:37:29.130 --> 00:37:56.605
Much remained: the Philistine districts and their five rulers at Gaza, Ashdod, Ashkelon, Gath, and Ekron, along with the Avvim; territory from Shihor near Egypt toward Ekron; the land of the Geshurites; Canaanite and Sidonian regions toward Aphek; the land of the Gebalites; Lebanon from Baal-gad below Hermon toward the entrance of Hamath; and the hill country toward Misrephoth-maim.

00:37:56.785 --> 00:38:14.735
Joshua was not ordered to complete every campaign before assigning territory. God promised that the remaining inhabitants would be driven out and directed Joshua to apportion the land to the nine and a half tribes still awaiting their western inheritances.

00:38:14.915 --> 00:38:28.465
A map could acknowledge a future task without pretending that task had been accomplished. The distribution began by recording the territories Moses had already assigned east of the Jordan.

00:38:28.645 --> 00:38:50.695
Reuben received land beginning near Aroer at the Arnon, across the plateau near Medeba and Dibon, and through Heshbon's former dominion. Its towns included Bamoth-baal, Beth-baal-meon, Jahaz, Kedemoth, Mephaath, Kiriathaim, Sibmah, Zereth-shahar, Beth-peor, the slopes of Pisgah and Beth-jeshimoth.

00:38:50.875 --> 00:39:05.925
The eastern campaigns had also defeated Midianite chiefs Evi, Rekem, Zur, Hur and Reba, and Balaam son of Beor, the diviner. Reuben's inheritance reached the Jordan, with its towns and villages.

00:39:06.105 --> 00:39:27.680
Gad received Jazer and cities of Gilead, land reaching toward Aroer near Rabbah, then through Heshbon, Ramath-mizpeh, Betonim and Mahanaim. In the valley its territory included Beth-haram, Beth-nimrah, Succoth and Zaphon, with the eastern Jordan boundary running toward the Sea of Chinneroth.

00:39:27.860 --> 00:39:48.035
The half-tribe of Manasseh received its eastern allotment around Mahanaim, Bashan, and Og's former kingdom, including the sixty settlements of Jair, half of Gilead, Ashtaroth and Edrei. Portions belonged to descendants of Machir, son of Manasseh. There were qualifications.

00:39:48.215 --> 00:40:04.840
Israel had not expelled the Geshurites and Maacathites; they continued to live among the Israelites in the region. And there was one tribe for which no comparable territory was set aside. Levi received no broad tribal inheritance.

00:40:05.020 --> 00:40:25.870
The book repeats the explanation in two forms: the offerings made to Israel's God, and God Himself, were Levi's inheritance. These eastern distributions had been made by Moses on the plains of Moab, across from Jericho. Joshua's task was to finish the allocation on the west.

00:40:26.050 --> 00:40:42.725
The distinction between promise and possession had become central. Joshua could apportion land that Israel had not fully occupied. The inheritance was real in the covenantal account; the work of making it home remained incomplete.

00:40:43.425 --> 00:40:57.625
The book says explicitly that Joshua was elderly while “very much land” remained. That prevents the broad conquest summaries from being misread as claims that every settlement was already under Israelite control.

00:40:57.805 --> 00:41:14.780
The eastern allotments contain extensive town lists because territory was defined through specific places, boundaries, and former jurisdictions. Levi's lack of a territorial inheritance recurs throughout the allocation narrative; it is not an oversight.
